<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim Shah Sharqi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1440">1440</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim Shah Sharqi was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> from 1402 to 1440, the greatest ruler of the Sharqi dynasty. He welcomed scholars fleeing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, making Jaunpur known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atala-mosque">Atala Mosque</a> in a distinctive style with massive gateways. He fought the Delhi sultans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah-sharqi.jpg" alt="Ibrahim Shah Sharqi" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaunpur_Sultante,_Shams_al-Din_Ibrahim_Shah,_dated_1438.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ibrahim, who took the regnal name Shams-ud-Din Ibrahim Shah, was the third sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>. He and his elder brother Mubarak had been adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-sarwar">Malik Sarwar</a>, the founder of the sultanate. Mubarak succeeded Malik Sarwar in 1399 as Mubarak Shah, and on his death Ibrahim took the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign Ibrahim was drawn into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a>, where Malik Arslan had killed its ruler Ganesvara and seized the land in 1402. Ganesvara's successor Kirti Singh turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>, and a Sharqi army under the general Malik Muhammad Ghani crossed the Gandak River and defeated and killed Arslan in the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raipur">Raipur</a>. In October and November 1406 Ibrahim marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>; after a feigned withdrawal he besieged its fort for four months until Malik Mahmud Tarmati surrendered. An advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in October 1407 took Sambhal and Baran, but he turned back when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> moved towards Jaunpur.</p><p>In 1415, at the call of the saint Qutb-ul-Alam, who accused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ganesha">Raja Ganesha</a> of oppressing Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Ibrahim defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a>; Ganesha's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah">Jadu</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and took the throne, and Ibrahim, on the saint's advice, withdrew without annexing Bengal. In a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in 1416 he drove Gaj Raj and his brother from Karoor. From 1433 he besieged Mahmudabad and fought Hushang Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> over it, and in 1437 he annexed large parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, until Sultan Muhammad Shah made peace and gave his daughter Bibi Raji in marriage to Ibrahim's son Mahmud Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ibrahim ruled until his death in 1440, and historians often regard his reign as the golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> campaign came about ten years of peace, when scholars from many parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> settled in the city. Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atala-mosque">Atala Masjid</a>, the Chahar Ungli Masjid and the Jhanjhari Masjid were built in his time. He upheld Islamic law and theology and oversaw the writing of many scholarly works, and is remembered as a warrior and patron of the arts and learning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibrahim Shah of Jaunpur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibrahim Shah Sharqi</b> made Jaunpur a refuge for scholars fleeing Delhi, earning it the name the Shiraz of India.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaunpur-sultanate">Jaunpur Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
